course: dictionary
lesson: 1440
title: Carrion: Nature's Clean-Up Crew
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Carrion is the dead and rotting flesh of animals. It is a word you cannot count, like sand or water, so you say some carrion, not two carrions.
explain: Animals that eat carrion are called scavengers. Vultures, crows, hyenas, Tasmanian devils and burying beetles all feed on it, and they help clean up nature and recycle nutrients back into the ground.
explain: Carrion can also be an adjective. A carrion crow is a crow that eats dead animals. Context clues help here: «The titan arum... smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.» Rotting meat is the clue, so carrion means dead, rotting flesh.
fact: Carrion means the dead and rotting flesh of animals.
fact: A scavenger is an animal that feeds on carrion.
fact: Vultures, crows, hyenas, Tasmanian devils and burying beetles all eat carrion.
fact: Scavengers help clean up nature and recycle nutrients.
fact: Some flowers, called carrion flowers, smell like carrion to attract flies.
exercise: «The vulture circled high and then landed beside the carrion.» What is carrion in this sentence? | A kind of stone | Rain that falls in spring | Fresh green grass | *Dead and rotting flesh of animals
exercise: Which of these animals is a scavenger that feeds on carrion? | Bee | *Hyena | Dolphin | Rabbit
exercise: «The titan arum... smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.» Why does the flower smell like this? | To make the air smell sweet for visitors | *To call carrion beetles and flies | To wake up the bees | To scare away the crows
test: Is carrion a word you can count, like one carrion or two carrions? | *No, it is uncountable, like sand or water | Yes, you say one carrion and two carrions | Only in the plural form | Yes, but only for birds
test: Which phrase names a flower that smells like rotting meat? | Carrion crow | Rotting meadow | Scavenger flower | *Carrion flower
exam: Which sentence uses carrion correctly? | The carrion sky was bright and sunny | *The hyena fed on the carrion left by the lions | The children sang a carrion song at assembly | She ate a carrion sandwich for lunch
